{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Expression.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Expression.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"regular expression regular expression","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Expression.md","fileName":"Regular Expression.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Regular Expression","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Regular Languages and Finite Automata]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Languages and Finite Automata","Kleene Star","Formal Language","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Expression.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Expression.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"regular expression regular expression","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Expression.md","fileName":"Regular Expression.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Regular Expression","heading":"Regular Expression","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A regular expression is built from ∅, ε, alphabet symbols, union, concatenation, [[Kleene Star]], and grouping. It generates a [[Formal Language]] by interpreting those constructors as the corresponding operations on sets of strings.\n\nA language is regular when some regular expression generates it. Practical search patterns add conveniences such as character classes, optional items, and one-or-more repetition, but features such as backreferences can exceed the power of formal regular expressions.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Languages and Finite Automata","Kleene Star","Formal Language","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Expression.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Regular Expression.md","sourceTyp